+### Read aloud on the result pages, 29–30 Oct 2026 (branch `feat/demo-polish`)
+
+Row 11 of the owner's feature table. The owner asked for the Simple/Detailed switch to open on Simple "with voice-only interface", because the readers are rural and many cannot read. Simple became the default (`7fdb08c`). Voice-only was declined. The part of it that can be built honestly is a Listen button under the light on `/analysis` and `/advisory`, which reads the verdict aloud.
+
+- **Why not voice-only.** Voice exists only on `/talk`, which is optional and off unless the server and the build both turn the helper on, and nothing read a result aloud. The notice and the consent tick are an explicit act that a voice-only flow cannot show. The 7 non-English notices are machine-written and unread (S16), Malayalam has no speech model, and Bhashini had not issued a key.
+- **Only a voice on the device is used.** The browser's engine may hand a sentence to a voice it reaches over the network, which would send text to a server the notice does not name. `localVoiceFor` accepts a voice only when `localService` is `true`, compares the language part of its tag whole (`hil-PH` is not Hindi; Android's `hi_IN` reads as `hi-IN`), takes the same region when there is one and otherwise the first local voice of that language. With none, `ReadAloud` draws nothing. `speak.js` opens no connection, and `speak-lib-test` greps its source for the usual ways to open one.
+- **The finding: the author's own browser has no such voice.** Edge on the owner's Windows laptop listed 321 voices on 29 Oct 2026 and every one was a network voice, so the button does not appear there in any language. That is the intended behaviour and a risk for the video. The ways round it are all untested: record on an Android phone, add a Hindi voice in Windows Settings (Time & language, Speech, Add voices), or record that beat in English. `docs/HACKATHON.md` §10.1 step 9 gives the console probe. Do not claim that Chrome on Android has a Hindi voice: nobody has looked.
+- **What is spoken.** The light's word (`band.green`, `band.yellow` or `band.red`), the `band.basis` sentence that says what the light is not, and the page's own two sentences (`/analysis`: the headline and the next step; `/advisory`: the takeaway card's two). A result stored before the band existed has none, and speaks the two sentences alone. Each line is its own utterance, because some engines are reported to stop a long one part-way (not seen here). A line with a digit in any script is left out, not read: an engine may say a digit string wrongly, and the verdict sentences carry none. `ra.note` says what the button does and is shown, not spoken. Nothing starts without a press.
+- **Stopping.** A second press, a language switch, the voice being taken away and leaving the page each stop the speech. The cleanup is keyed on the voice's name and language and not on the object, because a browser that lists its voices again may hand back new objects for the same voices, and that must not cut the speech. The queue marks itself dead on a stop, so the error events a cancelled line raises a moment later cannot end a run that replaced it. `cancel()` is called before `speak()` only when something is playing, because a browser can drop the first utterance when `speak` follows `cancel` at once.
+- **What changed.** Two new files (`audio/speak.js`, `components/ReadAloud.jsx`), a call on each of the two pages (`AdvisoryPage` names the takeaway's two sentences once so the card and the button use the same), and 3 keys × 8 languages (`ra.listen`, `ra.stop`, `ra.note`), which makes 901 keys and 7,208 values. The backend is untouched and the suite stays at 2,189. The `ra.*` sets are machine-written and unreviewed (rows 216 to 218 of `docs/HINDI_REVIEW.md`). There is no setting to turn the button off.
+- **How it was checked.** `speak-lib-test.mjs` (23 `node --test` tests; a CI step, not yet run on Linux). The gate `tools/verify/read-aloud.mjs` swaps `speechSynthesis` for a fake before the page loads and needs Vite only: 99 page loads, 750 checks, 13 scenarios, 20 finding kinds; exit 0, no finding and no fault on the finished tree. Its fake-engine refuters (`--broken=cancel`, `=local`, `=digits`) report 6, 53 and 89 findings and exit 1, the bare `--broken` 146, none unrefuted. `tools/README.md`, "The Listen button.", has the scenario list.
+- **Twenty planted defects, and the one kind nothing had tripped.** Defects were planted in `speak.js` and `ReadAloud.jsx` one at a time (the `localService` test removed, the digit filter removed, the cleanup removed, speech started on mount, a `fetch` on press, the language matched by prefix, and so on); each run edited the file, ran the gate, put the file back and compared its sha256. The first 19 all exited 1 with the kind planted for, but `COVERED` (something laid over the button, so a press never lands) was the one of the 20 finding kinds none of them reached, so it had never been seen to fire. A 20th plant, a `relative` block with an `absolute inset-0` span over the button, fired it 70 times in 211 findings of eight kinds. That is the CLAUDE.md rule again: a check that reports zero has not been shown to work until the thing it watches is broken on purpose. The kinds beyond the planted one in a row are the same fault seen in other scenarios; the table is in `tools/README.md`, and the script that made the edits is kept outside the repo.
+- **Still open.** No real speech engine has been driven: the gate uses a fake and the author's laptop has no local voice, so how a real voice sounds and the order of a real engine's events are unobserved. The `ra.*` wording is unreviewed. `read-aloud` is Windows-only and not in CI.
